WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Have you ever wondered why we dream?
你有没有想过我们为什么会做梦？

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Every night, when we close our eyes and drift off to sleep, our brains embark on a remarkable journey through the world of dreams.
每天晚上，当我们闭上眼睛进入梦乡时，我们的大脑开始了一段穿越梦境世界的非凡旅程。

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
This mysterious phenomenon has fascinated scientists, philosophers, and ordinary people for thousands of years.
这个神秘的现象几千年来一直让科学家、哲学家和普通人着迷。

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
During sleep, our brain does not simply shut down.
在睡眠期间，我们的大脑并不会简单地关闭。

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Instead, it goes through several distinct stages, each with its own unique characteristics.
相反，它会经历几个不同的阶段，每个阶段都有其独特的特征。

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The sleep cycle typically repeats every ninety minutes, and we experience four to six cycles per night.
睡眠周期通常每九十分钟重复一次，我们每晚经历四到六个周期。

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
These stages include light sleep, deep sleep, and REM sleep, which stands for Rapid Eye Movement.
这些阶段包括浅睡眠、深睡眠和 REM 睡眠，即快速眼动睡眠。

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Scientists believe that dreams serve multiple important functions.
科学家认为，梦有多种重要功能。

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
First, they help us process and consolidate memories.
首先，它们帮助我们处理和巩固记忆。

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Throughout the day, we gather enormous amounts of information.
一整天下来，我们收集了大量的信息。

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
During sleep, especially during REM sleep, our brain sorts through this information, deciding what to keep and what to discard.
在睡眠期间，特别是在快速眼动睡眠期间，我们的大脑整理这些信息，决定保留什么、丢弃什么。

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
This is why sleep is crucial for learning and memory.
这就是为什么睡眠对于学习和记忆至关重要。

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Research shows that people who are deprived of REM sleep have difficulty remembering new information and solving problems.
研究表明，被剥夺快速眼动睡眠的人在记忆新信息和解决问题方面有困难。

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
In one study, participants who were allowed to complete full sleep cycles performed significantly better on memory tests than those who were woken up during REM sleep.
在一项研究中，被允许完成完整睡眠周期的参与者在记忆测试中的表现明显优于那些在快速眼动睡眠期间被叫醒的人。

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Another theory suggests that dreams help us process emotions.
另一种理论认为，梦帮助我们处理情绪。

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
During dreaming, our brain creates simulated scenarios that allow us to work through emotional experiences from the day.
在做梦期间，我们的大脑创造模拟场景，让我们能够处理一天中的情感体验。

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.000
This emotional processing may help us wake up feeling more balanced and ready to face new challenges.
这种情感处理可能帮助我们醒来时感觉更加平衡，准备好面对新的挑战。

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.000
The subconscious mind becomes highly active during dreaming.
潜意识在做梦期间变得高度活跃。

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
While our conscious mind rests, the subconscious creates elaborate stories, often combining recent experiences with old memories in unexpected ways.
当我们的意识休息时，潜意识创造复杂的故事，经常将最近的经历与旧的记忆以意想不到的方式结合起来。

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
This is why dreams can feel so strange yet somehow meaningful.
这就是为什么梦可能感觉如此奇怪，却又不知何故地有意义。

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Some researchers propose that dreaming serves an evolutionary purpose.
一些研究人员提出，做梦有进化目的。

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
By simulating threatening scenarios in our dreams, we may be practicing responses to danger, preparing ourselves to handle real threats when we are awake.
通过在梦中模拟威胁性场景，我们可能在练习应对危险的方式，为我们在清醒时处理真正的威胁做好准备。

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Sleep and dreaming are essential for our physical and mental health.
睡眠和做梦对我们的身心健康至关重要。

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
People who do not get enough quality sleep often experience mood problems, difficulty concentrating, and weakened immune systems.
没有获得足够优质睡眠的人经常会出现情绪问题、注意力难以集中和免疫系统减弱。

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.000
It is important to prioritize sleep as part of a healthy lifestyle.
将睡眠作为健康生活方式的一部分来重视是很重要的。

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.000
In conclusion, while we still do not fully understand every aspect of dreaming, science has revealed that it plays vital roles in memory consolidation, emotional processing, and overall brain health.
总之，虽然我们仍然没有完全了解做梦的方方面面，但科学已经揭示，它在记忆巩固、情感处理和整体大脑健康方面发挥着至关重要的作用。

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
The next time you drift off to sleep, remember that your brain is about to begin one of its most important and fascinating activities.
下次当你入睡时，请记住你的大脑即将开始它最重要、最迷人的活动之一。

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Sweet dreams!
祝你好梦！
